WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.549
- [Host] This story begins in California.

00:00:03.549 --> 00:00:08.037
To be more specific, here,
in an unspecified studio lot.

00:00:08.037 --> 00:00:12.189
This is the story of a
TV producer, a physicist,

00:00:12.189 --> 00:00:13.022
and the man who brought
them both together.

00:00:13.022 --> 00:00:16.355
(calm orchestral music)

00:00:21.117 --> 00:00:22.216
That's Wendy.

00:00:22.216 --> 00:00:24.945
- I'm one of the co-producers
of Marvel's Agent Carter.

00:00:24.945 --> 00:00:28.944
- [Host] In 2015, ABC
released the show Agent Carter

00:00:28.944 --> 00:00:32.785
about a secret agent
working in the late 1940s.

00:00:32.785 --> 00:00:36.386
In season two, they debuted a
character named Jason Wilkes.

00:00:36.386 --> 00:00:38.946
- Jason is a scientist who
works at Isodyne Energy.

00:00:38.946 --> 00:00:41.256
- [Host] And a science
character needs to sound

00:00:41.256 --> 00:00:44.338
like a real scientist, kind of like this.

00:00:44.338 --> 00:00:46.957
- Peggy enters to find Howard
at the chalkboard with Wilkes.

00:00:46.957 --> 00:00:48.123
- Lambda is given...
- Lambda is given

00:00:48.123 --> 00:00:50.810
by Planck's Constant divided by MC --

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:52.290
- [Howard] Except we can't calculate M

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:53.941
without an accurate reading of your mass.

00:00:53.941 --> 00:00:55.858
No mass, no wavelength.

00:00:58.152 --> 00:01:00.552
- [Host] So you need to
write science-y dialogue,

00:01:00.552 --> 00:01:02.912
write science-y stuff on the chalkboard,

00:01:02.912 --> 00:01:05.059
and have a science-y looking lab,

00:01:05.059 --> 00:01:07.900
but you don't have any
science writers on staff?

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.650
- No, unfortunately we do not.

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:10.861
- [Host] So, what did you do?

00:01:10.861 --> 00:01:12.379
- We called our friends up at the

00:01:12.379 --> 00:01:14.181
National Academy of Sciences.

00:01:14.181 --> 00:01:15.410
(phone ringing)

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:17.490
- Idea Exchange, it's Rick.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:19.832
- [Narrator] This is Rick.

00:01:19.832 --> 00:01:21.061
- Hey hey. I'm Rick.

00:01:21.061 --> 00:01:22.781
I work at the National
Academy of Sciences.

00:01:22.781 --> 00:01:24.290
We connect scientists and engineers

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:25.501
with people in Hollywood.

00:01:25.501 --> 00:01:26.850
- [Host] People like Wendy?

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:30.300
- Yeah, we connected her with Clifford.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.590
- Hi.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.112
My name's Clifford Johnson.

00:01:33.112 --> 00:01:35.930
I'm a professor in the Physics
and Astronomy Department

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:37.202
at USC.

00:01:37.202 --> 00:01:38.629
- [Host] So it goes like this,

00:01:38.629 --> 00:01:40.212
Rick pairs Clifford

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:42.522
with Wendy.

00:01:42.522 --> 00:01:44.541
And they work together
to bring real science

00:01:44.541 --> 00:01:45.708
into TV shows.

00:01:46.861 --> 00:01:47.694
- Yeah.

00:01:47.694 --> 00:01:48.561
- Yeah.

00:01:48.561 --> 00:01:49.394
- Yeah.

00:01:51.392 --> 00:01:53.530
For example, I got an
email one time saying,

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:56.659
"So how do you build an atomic bomb?"

00:01:56.659 --> 00:02:00.070
And I had to sort of help them with that.

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:01.472
It was a lot of fun.

00:02:01.472 --> 00:02:03.570
- [Host] Rick's team has
a database with a lot

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.901
of scientists in it.

00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:07.389
Over 2,000.

00:02:07.389 --> 00:02:10.850
And they've worked on some
major blockbuster films.

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.979
- [Rick] Iron Man Two and Three.

00:02:12.979 --> 00:02:14.500
The Avengers: Age of Ultron.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.592
Batman versus Superman.

00:02:15.592 --> 00:02:17.000
Star Trek Into Darkness.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.320
Mission Impossible Five.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:21.962
Films and narrative have
been inspiring people

00:02:21.962 --> 00:02:23.795
since the 19th Century

00:02:24.733 --> 00:02:27.282
to go out and do great things

00:02:27.282 --> 00:02:29.072
and build great things.

00:02:29.072 --> 00:02:30.842
We're not trying to the
be the accuracy police,

00:02:30.842 --> 00:02:33.052
we're not fact checkers per say.

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:36.610
What our goal is, is to inspire
more scientist characters

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:40.120
and better science in
mainstream media projects.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:41.840
- [Host] And that's the
story about the hotline

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.181
for Scientists in Hollywood.
(phone ringing)

00:02:44.181 --> 00:02:46.592
- Idea Exchange, it's Rick.

00:02:46.592 --> 00:02:49.175
(upbeat music)

